out.push(A('⚠ **Item 4 is the one that separates a class: _of the eleven paragraphs, each carries a figure._** **_Each_ stands alone as a pronoun, _every_ cannot, and _each of_ needs _them_.** **Teach the three together, because every student in the room uses all three and about half of them think _every_ is simply the plural of _each_.** ⚠ **And item 2 is subject-verb agreement after a negative pronoun**: **_nothing_ is singular and takes _needs_**, and the error is produced by _the paragraph_ sitting next to the verb — **which is the same fault as Unit 3\'s _what neither of them show_, met for the second time and worth naming as a pattern rather than a rule.**'));
out.push(A('**B  Listening** **1** facts   **2** four   **3** six   **4** end   **5** inaction _(or_ doing nothing _)_   **6** eleven   **7** put back   **8** examined'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **Part 2 is sentence completion and the answers are the speaker\'s own words, which is the single most useful thing to tell a class.** **Candidates lose marks by paraphrasing a word they heard perfectly well.** **Item 5 is the one where this happens most** — **the recording says _the cost of inaction_ and a candidate who writes _the cost of not acting_ has heard it correctly and will not be given the mark.** ⚠ **And item 2 is the trap of the set**, because the recording contains two numbers close together: forty-one instances and four pages. **The sentence asks for the length of the report, and about a third of any class writes _forty-one_** — **which is the commonest Part 2 error at every level: the number that was loudest, rather than the number the sentence asked for.** **Teach the mechanical fix: underline the grammatical slot before the recording starts, and here it is _a report of ______ pages_, which cannot take forty-one.**'));
